From: aswin-mcw <aswin.kalies@multicorewareinc.com>
Subject: [PATCH] armcap: aarch64 Cygwin: ask Windows for the CPU features

On aarch64 Cygwin OPENSSL_cpuid_setup() took the generic path, which
probes instructions under a SIGILL handler from a library constructor.
That works when libcrypto is linked at startup, but Cygwin cannot deliver
a signal raised while a DLL is being loaded by dlopen(): the process
dies.  Perl's Net::SSLeay (and anything else that dlopen()s libcrypto)
could not be loaded at all.

Use the native Windows path instead, as the mingw targets do:
IsProcessorFeaturePresent() needs no probing, and OPENSSL_cpuid_setup()
is still called from OPENSSL_init_crypto().

Signed-off-by: aswin-mcw <aswin.kalies@multicorewareinc.com>
--- a/crypto/armcap.c
+++ b/crypto/armcap.c
@@ -18,9 +18,19 @@
 #include <signal.h>
 #endif
 #include "internal/cryptlib.h"
-#ifdef _WIN32
+/*
+ * aarch64 Cygwin asks Windows for the CPU features, as native Windows does:
+ * the SIGILL probe below runs in the library constructor, and Cygwin cannot
+ * deliver a signal raised while a DLL is being loaded by dlopen() (perl's
+ * Net::SSLeay, for one, loads libcrypto that way); the process dies.
+ */
+#if defined(__CYGWIN__) && defined(__aarch64__)
+# define OSSL_ARMCAP_FROM_WINDOWS
+#endif
+#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(OSSL_ARMCAP_FROM_WINDOWS)
 #include <windows.h>
-#else
+#endif
+#ifndef _WIN32
 #include <unistd.h>
 #endif
 #include "arm_arch.h"
@@ -29,7 +39,7 @@
 unsigned int OPENSSL_arm_midr = 0;
 unsigned int OPENSSL_armv8_rsa_neonized = 0;
 
-#ifdef _WIN32
+#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(OSSL_ARMCAP_FROM_WINDOWS)
 void OPENSSL_cpuid_setup(void)
 {
     OPENSSL_armcap_P |= ARMV7_NEON;
